WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:04.210
If you need something translated from one
language into another, what resource do you

00:00:04.210 --> 00:00:05.240
turn to?

00:00:05.240 --> 00:00:06.570
Google Translate, of course!

00:00:06.570 --> 00:00:11.519
Since its launch way back in 2006, there have
been few translation services that are as

00:00:11.519 --> 00:00:16.030
widely used, by both language learners and
non-language learners alike.

00:00:16.030 --> 00:00:22.990
It is so popular, in fact, that in 2018, Google
reported that the service translates approximately

00:00:22.990 --> 00:00:27.340
143 billion words every day!

00:00:27.340 --> 00:00:29.369
But how good is Google Translate, really?

00:00:29.369 --> 00:00:33.530
Is it really the best tool to use if you need
a quick and accurate translation?

00:00:33.530 --> 00:00:37.480
Hi, my name is Luca from Luca Lampariello.com
and today I'm going to talk to you about the

00:00:37.480 --> 00:00:42.630
strengths and weaknesses of Google Translate
as a language learning tool, as well as all

00:00:42.630 --> 00:00:46.270
the ways in which you can use the service
to benefit your learning.

00:00:46.270 --> 00:00:49.100
So, what is the purpose of Google Translate
really?

00:00:49.100 --> 00:00:50.110
That much is obvious.

00:00:50.110 --> 00:00:54.539
Its purpose is to use a process called “machine
learning” to take text that is in one language,

00:00:54.539 --> 00:00:58.219
and quickly and reliably translate it into
another language.

00:00:58.219 --> 00:01:04.369
The system is not perfect, but in most cases
it delivers free, reasonably-accurate translations

00:01:04.369 --> 00:01:07.560
in a pinch, without having to rely on human
translators.

00:01:07.560 --> 00:01:08.889
It’s simple to use, too.

00:01:08.889 --> 00:01:13.350
All you need to do is type the text in your
“starting” language on the left side,

00:01:13.350 --> 00:01:17.770
and then pick your “destination” language
from the drop down list on the right side.

00:01:17.770 --> 00:01:23.709
Once you have done that, Google instantaneously
serves up the translated text on the right-side

00:01:23.709 --> 00:01:24.709
text box.

00:01:24.709 --> 00:01:25.709
Quick and easy!

00:01:25.709 --> 00:01:29.829
But how can we, as language learners, get
the most mileage out of Google Translate?

00:01:29.829 --> 00:01:34.149
I have a few key strategies, which I will
share with you now.

00:01:34.149 --> 00:01:36.020
Never translate single words

00:01:36.020 --> 00:01:41.409
This is more of a recommendation than a strategy,
but if you follow it, the quality of the translations

00:01:41.409 --> 00:01:44.680
you receive from Google Translate will be
much, much better:

00:01:44.680 --> 00:01:50.689
When you use Google Translate, never, ever
use it to translate single words!

00:01:50.689 --> 00:01:55.399
Behind the scenes of Google Translate, the
program is really a complex series of machine

00:01:55.399 --> 00:02:01.249
learning algorithms and neural networks that
have been trained to translate words and phrases

00:02:01.249 --> 00:02:02.380
based on context.

00:02:02.380 --> 00:02:06.990
In language learning terms, context refers
to the surrounding “environment” that

00:02:06.990 --> 00:02:10.679
a word or phrase finds itself in.

00:02:10.679 --> 00:02:15.180
Context is what is used to determine which
meaning of a word is the correct one in a

00:02:15.180 --> 00:02:18.950
specific situation, even if the word itself
has multiple meanings.

00:02:18.950 --> 00:02:23.290
That’s why, for example, when I say a phrase
like “birds can fly”, you know that the

00:02:23.290 --> 00:02:28.489
word “fly” is referring to the verb “to
fly”, rather than the pesky, buzzing insect

00:02:28.489 --> 00:02:33.239
known as a “fly”, or even the “fly”
found on a pair of pants.

00:02:33.239 --> 00:02:37.400
Machine translators need context to understand
language, just like humans do.

00:02:37.400 --> 00:02:41.730
If you type the word “fly” into Google
translate, it will have no idea if you’re

00:02:41.730 --> 00:02:45.530
specifically referring to the verb “to fly”,
or any of the other possible meanings.

00:02:45.530 --> 00:02:47.829
So it will just pick one for you.

00:02:47.829 --> 00:02:51.799
As a result, since you most likely don’t
understand the language that “fly” is

00:02:51.799 --> 00:02:55.959
being translated into, there’s a decent
likelihood you’ll end up with an incorrect

00:02:55.959 --> 00:02:58.940
translation without even realizing it.

00:02:58.940 --> 00:03:05.650
So again, please remember to always use multiple
word phrases or sentences when you input language

00:03:05.650 --> 00:03:06.939
into Google Translate.

00:03:06.939 --> 00:03:11.799
It’s the only way to ensure even a reasonably
accurate translation.

00:03:11.799 --> 00:03:12.849
2.

00:03:12.849 --> 00:03:15.829
Create Instant, Bilingual Texts with Audio

00:03:15.829 --> 00:03:19.870
Although I just spoke about how you should
always make sure to throw at least one entire

00:03:19.870 --> 00:03:24.420
phrase or sentence into Google Translate at
a time, there’s actually nothing stopping

00:03:24.420 --> 00:03:27.220
you from translating texts that are much,
much larger.

00:03:27.220 --> 00:03:32.959
On its main “text” page, Google Translate
allows you to translate 5,000 characters at

00:03:32.959 --> 00:03:33.959
a time.

00:03:33.959 --> 00:03:37.019
That’s more than the amount of characters
you would need to transcribe everything I’ve

00:03:37.019 --> 00:03:38.829
said in this entire video.

00:03:38.829 --> 00:03:43.150
However, if you have an even larger text to
translate, you can turn to Google Translate’s

00:03:43.150 --> 00:03:47.890
Documents tab, which allows you to upload
entire text documents to be translated.

00:03:47.890 --> 00:03:52.470
Why is it useful for Google Translate to be
able to translate entire documents at a time?

00:03:52.470 --> 00:03:59.640
Because this means we can use it to create
bilingual texts with audio, all in a moment’s

00:03:59.640 --> 00:04:00.640
notice.

00:04:00.640 --> 00:04:01.779
How do you do this?

00:04:01.779 --> 00:04:04.099
First you need a text in your target language.

00:04:04.099 --> 00:04:09.090
You should ALWAYS start with an authentic
target language text, so that you are not

00:04:09.090 --> 00:04:12.069
learning from incorrect or inaccurate language.

00:04:12.069 --> 00:04:17.890
When you have your text, you can type, paste,
or upload it to Google Translate, and have

00:04:17.890 --> 00:04:20.780
the system translate that text into your mother
tongue.

00:04:20.780 --> 00:04:22.509
A few moments later and...voila!

00:04:22.509 --> 00:04:27.740
You’ve now got a bilingual version of your
original text, in both your target language

00:04:27.740 --> 00:04:29.289
and your native language.

00:04:29.289 --> 00:04:33.000
You can then use a resource like this to read
through the original “foreign” version,

00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:37.580
using the translated version as a guide when
you get lost, or want to know the definition

00:04:37.580 --> 00:04:38.580
of a word.

00:04:38.580 --> 00:04:42.680
I’ve been using this process recently in
my Danish learning, and it has been amazingly

00:04:42.680 --> 00:04:43.680
helpful:

00:04:43.680 --> 00:04:47.360
First, I grab the subtitles from a video on
my favorite Danish YouTube channel, called

00:04:47.360 --> 00:04:49.530
WTF er det?

00:04:49.530 --> 00:04:55.260
- yes, WTF really stands for what the f***-
Then, I simply copy the text, paste it into

00:04:55.260 --> 00:05:00.450
Google Translate and I’ve instantly got
an interesting and enjoyable learning resource.

00:05:00.450 --> 00:05:04.199
There is one problem with creating bilingual
texts with Google Translate, though, which

00:05:04.199 --> 00:05:06.330
you may have already thought of:

00:05:06.330 --> 00:05:10.680
Google Translate’s translations are often
decently accurate, but not accurate enough

00:05:10.680 --> 00:05:15.319
to be considered perfect, or at least equal
to what a human translator would give you.

00:05:15.319 --> 00:05:19.430
Though this may seem worrisome at first, we
need to remember the reason why we’re using

00:05:19.430 --> 00:05:23.580
a machine translator in the first place: to
get a free and quick translation that’s

00:05:23.580 --> 00:05:26.090
at the very least comprehensible.

00:05:26.090 --> 00:05:29.479
The translation into your native language
does not have to be perfect.

00:05:29.479 --> 00:05:33.480
It has to be good enough for you to understand
the text in your target language.

00:05:33.480 --> 00:05:38.110
In this regard, I think that Google Translate
does a respectable job, especially compared

00:05:38.110 --> 00:05:42.250
to other translators, and even earlier versions
of Google Translate itself.

00:05:42.250 --> 00:05:45.750
If you’re still worried about getting as
accurate a translation as possible, there

00:05:45.750 --> 00:05:48.509
are a few things you can do:

00:05:48.509 --> 00:05:51.240
Translate simple texts
Translate between “close” languages.

00:05:51.240 --> 00:05:52.240
Translate simple texts

00:05:52.240 --> 00:05:57.259
The more complicated a text is, the more likely
Google Translate will have a hard time translating

00:05:57.259 --> 00:05:58.259
it.

00:05:58.259 --> 00:06:02.669
So, as much as you’d like to use the service
to get a quick and free translation of Dostoyevsky’s

00:06:02.669 --> 00:06:06.340
The Brothers Karamazov (Бра́тья Карама́зовы),
the end result probably isn’t going to be

00:06:06.340 --> 00:06:07.600
very useful to you.

00:06:07.600 --> 00:06:11.550
To get as accurate a translation as possible,
you should stick to simple texts, like the

00:06:11.550 --> 00:06:16.570
ones normally written for the benefit of beginner
and Intermediate language learners.

00:06:16.570 --> 00:06:21.780
Anything more complex (like slang, academic
articles, or literature), and the translations

00:06:21.780 --> 00:06:23.480
might look like gibberish.

00:06:23.480 --> 00:06:24.480
Translate between close languages

00:06:24.480 --> 00:06:27.460
There are certain linguistic features that
can make it easier for Google Translate to

00:06:27.460 --> 00:06:30.680
give you a reliable translation between two
languages.

00:06:30.680 --> 00:06:35.580
The most useful of these is probably syntax,
which is the set of rules that govern the

00:06:35.580 --> 00:06:37.680
order of words in a sentence.

00:06:37.680 --> 00:06:42.570
Languages that share the same order of elements
(the order in which grammatical subjects,

00:06:42.570 --> 00:06:48.030
objects, and verbs appear, for example) can
be easily translated back and forth.

00:06:48.030 --> 00:06:51.851
Languages that are “genetically” related
usually share the same syntax, so if you are

00:06:51.851 --> 00:06:56.120
using two languages from the same language
family (or even a larger grouping), your ultimate

00:06:56.120 --> 00:06:58.090
translation will be much better.

00:06:58.090 --> 00:07:03.689
In this way, it is very easy for Google Translate
to translate reliably from Spanish to Italian,

00:07:03.689 --> 00:07:07.110
Italian to English, English to Polish, and
so on.

00:07:07.110 --> 00:07:11.550
Even unrelated languages that have the same
word order (like English and Chinese) can

00:07:11.550 --> 00:07:14.770
be translated more accurately between each
other.

00:07:14.770 --> 00:07:19.580
But what if you’re learning a language like
Japanese, and your native language is English?

00:07:19.580 --> 00:07:21.599
You’re not entirely out of luck.

00:07:21.599 --> 00:07:25.849
Google Translate’s ability to translate
between syntactically different languages

00:07:25.849 --> 00:07:31.879
(English/Japanese, Italian/Korean, etc) has
improved dramatically in recent years, but

00:07:31.879 --> 00:07:36.740
in general, the translations still remain
quite flawed, and often confusing.

00:07:36.740 --> 00:07:40.949
Fortunately, though, there are a number of
other translation products available that

00:07:40.949 --> 00:07:45.659
do a better job at translating between these
specific languages, and many of those have

00:07:45.659 --> 00:07:48.129
the same features as Google Translate.

00:07:48.129 --> 00:07:51.780
I’ve left a link in the description box
with more information.

00:07:51.780 --> 00:07:56.180
Now, the cool thing about Google Translate
is that it does not limit itself to providing

00:07:56.180 --> 00:07:57.849
just the mere translation.

00:07:57.849 --> 00:08:02.909
It also offers a transliteration of languages
with non latin scripts AND you have a function

00:08:02.909 --> 00:08:04.560
to read the text out loud.

00:08:04.560 --> 00:08:10.409
Yes, yes, it is still a bit robotic and gives
away to weird results, but it overall provides

00:08:10.409 --> 00:08:11.800
good comprehensible input.

00:08:11.800 --> 00:08:16.530
In other words, it allows you to transform
a completely foreign and alien text into something

00:08:16.530 --> 00:08:20.340
you can read, listen to and understand.

00:08:20.340 --> 00:08:22.509
Prepare Yourself for a Target Language Conversation

00:08:22.509 --> 00:08:26.389
Another engaging and smart way to use Google
Translate is to use it to prepare speaking

00:08:26.389 --> 00:08:28.900
sessions with your tutor or language partner.

00:08:28.900 --> 00:08:33.650
When you have scheduled time for a conversation,
be sure to spend at least twenty minutes beforehand

00:08:33.650 --> 00:08:34.820
preparing yourself.

00:08:34.820 --> 00:08:38.920
You can do this by using Google translate
to research words and phrases that will be

00:08:38.920 --> 00:08:41.160
useful in your upcoming conversation.

00:08:41.160 --> 00:08:44.880
Let’s assume that you and your tutor or
language partner do not have a conversation

00:08:44.880 --> 00:08:46.450
topic chosen in advance.

00:08:46.450 --> 00:08:49.770
As a result, you can decide to talk about
any topic under the sun.

00:08:49.770 --> 00:08:53.460
I’ve always found that it’s easiest and
most motivating to talk about topics that

00:08:53.460 --> 00:08:55.130
we care about.

00:08:55.130 --> 00:08:56.590
That motivate and interest us.

00:08:56.590 --> 00:08:59.810
That make us tick, so to speak.

00:08:59.810 --> 00:09:04.250
So pick a topic that you’re really passionate
about, and then imagine the kinds of things

00:09:04.250 --> 00:09:07.040
you can talk about when you bring that up
in conversation.

00:09:07.040 --> 00:09:11.150
You don’t have to do this in your target
language just yet—just imagine what a conversation

00:09:11.150 --> 00:09:13.390
on your chosen topic could look like.

00:09:13.390 --> 00:09:16.730
Then, try to simulate the conversation in
your target language.

00:09:16.730 --> 00:09:19.960
You can even play the role of your “partner”
too, if you’d like.

00:09:19.960 --> 00:09:23.560
As you do this, you’ll realize that there
are things that you want to say in your target

00:09:23.560 --> 00:09:26.080
language, that you don’t actually know how
to say.

00:09:26.080 --> 00:09:27.080
No worries!

00:09:27.080 --> 00:09:29.230
That’s what we need Google Translate for!

00:09:29.230 --> 00:09:32.500
Whenever you find that there’s something
in your simulated conversation that you want

00:09:32.500 --> 00:09:38.450
to say, but can’t, simply throw it into
Google Translate, and write down the result.

00:09:38.450 --> 00:09:42.440
Then, keep your list of “unknown” phrases
with you, and try to use them when you actually

00:09:42.440 --> 00:09:43.920
meet with your speaking partner.

00:09:43.920 --> 00:09:47.450
That’s what I do every time I have a conversation
with my Hungarian tutor.

00:09:47.450 --> 00:09:52.250
For example, I often love learning about astronomy,
cosmology, and space travel.

00:09:52.250 --> 00:09:57.200
So, whenever I learn a new language, this
is a topic I often try to talk about in that

00:09:57.200 --> 00:09:58.200
language.

00:09:58.200 --> 00:10:02.180
So one day, last year, I was meeting with
my Hungarian tutor, and decided to talk about

00:10:02.180 --> 00:10:03.180
the moon landing.

00:10:03.180 --> 00:10:05.130
That’s not an easy topic, as you can imagine.

00:10:05.130 --> 00:10:10.200
There were all sorts of words and phrases
that I would need to learn, but didn’t yet

00:10:10.200 --> 00:10:11.200
know.

00:10:11.200 --> 00:10:15.570
So I acted out my conversation alone, and
then turned to Google Translate.

00:10:15.570 --> 00:10:17.010
I typed in phrases like:

00:10:17.010 --> 00:10:20.410
“The Lunar module finally landed”
“The astronauts were tired”

00:10:20.410 --> 00:10:23.530
“Men walked on the moon”

00:10:23.530 --> 00:10:28.390
Notice how I don’t put single words, and
insead put phrases or short sentences.

00:10:28.390 --> 00:10:31.010
Remember the rule—no single words, ever!

00:10:31.010 --> 00:10:35.530
Once I had my translated phrases, I was able
to put them to use effectively when talking

00:10:35.530 --> 00:10:36.990
to my Hungarian tutor.

00:10:36.990 --> 00:10:40.980
Even though this technique often does often
go according to plan, it’s useful to remember

00:10:40.980 --> 00:10:43.580
that conversations are often unpredictable.

00:10:43.580 --> 00:10:47.560
Sometimes, your conversation will take an
unforeseen turn, and you’ll realize that

00:10:47.560 --> 00:10:51.840
there’s a phrase or two that you need, but
don’t know how to say in your target language.

00:10:51.840 --> 00:10:53.080
Again, no need to worry!

00:10:53.080 --> 00:10:57.580
As we’ve already mentioned, Google Translate
is super fast, so you can always just open

00:10:57.580 --> 00:11:02.000
it up on your computer or phone, and get a
quick translation on the spot.

00:11:02.000 --> 00:11:06.380
Since your tutor or language partner is with
you, you can also get immediate feedback on

00:11:06.380 --> 00:11:09.670
the correctness of the translated phrase or
sentence!

00:11:09.670 --> 00:11:13.490
Now, with that piece of advice, you might
be worried that using Google Translate during

00:11:13.490 --> 00:11:16.960
a conversation is like cheating on an academic
exam.

00:11:16.960 --> 00:11:18.730
Trust me, it’s nothing of the sort.

00:11:18.730 --> 00:11:23.600
If you use Google Translate in this way, you’re
using it to facilitate conversation, rather

00:11:23.600 --> 00:11:28.030
than allowing yourself to get stuck in those
awkward moments where you’re desperately

00:11:28.030 --> 00:11:31.400
trying to think of a word you need, but it
won’t come.

00:11:31.400 --> 00:11:32.400
Seriously.

00:11:32.400 --> 00:11:35.790
If you’re worried about this, I recommend
that you actually tell your partner or tutor

00:11:35.790 --> 00:11:38.780
upfront that you will be using Google Translate
for quick translation.

00:11:38.780 --> 00:11:44.270
There is freedom in truth, and no shame in
using technology to facilitate communication

00:11:44.270 --> 00:11:45.540
and help you speak better.

00:11:45.540 --> 00:11:51.850
Remember: a conversation (in most cases) is
not a level test, or a performance to be critiqued.

00:11:51.850 --> 00:11:56.760
It is a connection that benefits both people
the easier it naturally flows.

00:11:56.760 --> 00:11:58.130
So there you have it.

00:11:58.130 --> 00:12:01.550
These are just three ways in which you can
use Google Translate.

00:12:01.550 --> 00:12:02.800
To wrap it all up:

00:12:02.800 --> 00:12:07.110
For language learning, Google Translate is
neither a blessing, nor a curse.

00:12:07.110 --> 00:12:12.750
Rather, it is just a tool that when used correctly,
can help you quickly find decent-quality translations

00:12:12.750 --> 00:12:13.950
between two languages.

00:12:13.950 --> 00:12:19.820
You can use it to create bilingual comprehensible
texts, to prepare conversations and use it

00:12:19.820 --> 00:12:25.010
on the fly to help you find words and expressions
you don't know or still can’t use.

00:12:25.010 --> 00:12:27.950
All in all, I think Google Translate is a
great tool.

00:12:27.950 --> 00:12:31.640
It is free, easy to use and intuitive in most
situations.

00:12:31.640 --> 00:12:36.640
It does have its limitations, of course (especially
for certain combinations of languages) but

00:12:36.640 --> 00:12:39.450
the overall quality of the translations are
constantly improving.

00:12:39.450 --> 00:12:46.070
In any case, I think it is best used by beginner
or low-intermediate learners, and with combinations

00:12:46.070 --> 00:12:48.700
of languages that share a similar syntax.

00:12:48.700 --> 00:12:51.040
Now, how do you use Google Translate?

00:12:51.040 --> 00:12:55.630
Have you always used it in the same way, or
has your use of it changed over time?

00:12:55.630 --> 00:12:57.500
Let me know in the comments below

00:12:57.500 --> 00:13:03.680
Also, if you enjoyed the video, be sure to
like, subscribe and hit the bell to be notified

00:13:03.680 --> 00:13:06.310
whenever I publish another video on this channel.

00:13:06.310 --> 00:13:17.780
And as always, thanks for watching!

